[Cheonji Ilbo Chungbuk=Reporter Kim Heung-soon] Chungbuk Province is accelerating efforts to diversify its industrial structure away from a manufacturing-centered economy. Recognizing its vulnerability to global risks such as a downturn in the semiconductor industry, the province is focusing on fostering new industries and improving its economic structure. Semiconductor material and parts production is active in Cheongju and Osong, with the Chungbuk Regional Statistics Office reporting a 19.5% increase in manufacturing output compared to March of the previous year. Chungbuk is strategically fostering future growth engines such as artificial intelligence (AI), secondary batteries, and hydrogen. These efforts are aligned with national innovation platforms and policies targeting eight major new growth industries.

Experts expect that Chungbuk will establish a sustainable economic foundation through advanced manufacturing and convergence with tourism and cultural industries. Local residents also express hope for improved living infrastructure. Interest is growing in the province&rsquo;s economic direction. In particular, the professional science and technology and transportation industries are gaining attention due to industrial complex development and expanded logistics infrastructure, driving economic vitality. Chungbuk is strengthening the clustering of tech and logistics companies around national industrial complexes and leveraging its geographical advantage to become a logistics hub.

The demand for engineering, R&amp;D, and analytical services in the professional science and technology industry is growing with the advancement of high-tech manufacturing like semiconductors and secondary batteries. The transportation industry is thriving alongside increasing freight volumes from major complexes in Ochang and Osong. As smart logistics systems expand, efficiency is improving. Experts note that these two industries play a pivotal role in diversifying Chungbuk&rsquo;s economy, and strategic investment from both the government and local authorities is essential for sustained growth.

◆ Reality and Challenges of Professional Science and Technology and Transportation Industries

Professor Lee Tae-seong, adjunct professor at the Graduate School of Smart Ecological Industry Convergence at Chungbuk National University, attributes the growth of Chungbuk&rsquo;s professional science and technology sector to rising demand driven by advanced manufacturing, the clustering of tech companies around industrial complexes, and alignment with government R&amp;D policies and regional strategies.

He explained, &ldquo;As Chungbuk&rsquo;s high-value manufacturing in semiconductors, secondary batteries, and bio continues to develop, the demand for R&amp;D, design, analysis, and engineering services is continuously increasing. National industrial complexes and advanced clusters in areas like Ochang, Osong, and Cheongju are creating synergy through collaboration and talent inflow.&rdquo;

He added, &ldquo;With rising demand for ESG compliance and digital transformation, knowledge-based services like carbon emission diagnostics and AI-based production optimization are emerging as growth drivers. Chungbuk has solid talent supply foundations including Chungbuk National University, Korea National University of Transportation, and KAIST&rsquo;s Osong Campus. Long-term growth looks promising through the government&rsquo;s regional innovation platform and the Glocal University 30 project.&rdquo;

He emphasized that as Chungbuk&rsquo;s logistics hub development accelerates, the transportation industry is becoming a central logistics axis linking the Seoul metropolitan area with the rest of the country. &ldquo;Chungbuk&rsquo;s geographical advantage and expansion of logistics facilities within industrial complexes are facilitating corporate attraction and regional economic circulation,&rdquo; he stated. &ldquo;As a result, not only major logistics companies but also SMEs are growing, and infrastructure advancement and talent acquisition are emerging as key challenges for sustainable growth.&rdquo;

Experts including Professor Lee believe that these developments could drive a structural transformation of Chungbuk&rsquo;s overall economy.

◆ Achievements and Limitations of Construction and Equipment Investment


<img alt="Apartment construction site. (Source: Pinterest)" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202505/3268145_3332020_5717.jpg" />
Apartment construction site. (Source: Pinterest)



Professor Shin Yong-han, visiting professor at Seowon University, analyzed that fixed capital formation in Chungbuk increased by 2.3% year-on-year thanks to increased construction and equipment investment, surpassing the national average of 1.3%. He noted, &ldquo;This reflects the region&rsquo;s bright future outlook aligned with technological innovation and expanded R&amp;D investment.&rdquo;

However, he also pointed out challenges: &ldquo;As evidenced by the rehabilitation filing of Chungbuk&rsquo;s top-ranked construction firm &lsquo;D&rsquo; last month, the construction sector&rsquo;s outlook remains bleak and its limitations are clear.&rdquo;

He continued, &ldquo;In the first quarter alone, seven construction firms in Chungbuk closed&mdash;almost double the 10-year average. The industry is grappling with survival issues amid high interest rates, inflation, global economic slowdown, real estate market downturn, soaring construction costs, and political uncertainty.&rdquo;

Shin emphasized, &ldquo;Since construction has strong ripple effects across related industries such as steel, cement, transportation, and services, substantial financial support and institutional reform are urgently needed.&rdquo;

◆ New Industries: Achievements and Gaps


<img alt="The photo shows the development site of the National Advanced Industrial Complex for secondary batteries. (Provided by Chungcheongbuk-do Provincial Government) ⓒCheonji Ilbo, May 12, 2025." src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202505/3268145_3332022_5825.jpg" />
The photo shows the development site of the National Advanced Industrial Complex for secondary batteries. (Provided by Chungcheongbuk-do Provincial Government) ⓒCheonji Ilbo, May 12, 2025.



Chungbuk accounts for approximately 48% of Korea&rsquo;s secondary battery production, securing its position as a leading region. The province is pushing forward with the second hydrogen industry development plan through 2030, targeting four main strategies and 30 specific tasks. Integration of existing flagship industries with cutting-edge fields such as AI and quantum technology (CAB) is also active. While R&amp;D infrastructure such as Chungbuk National University&rsquo;s Quantum Research Center and the Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology (KAIST) Chungcheong Campus is being developed, challenges remain due to the lack of national research institutions and weak industry-academia collaboration.

Professor Lee noted, &ldquo;Chungbuk must focus on securing technological independence and nurturing talent. Maximizing synergy through convergence between CAB technologies and specialized industries like bio and semiconductors is crucial.&rdquo;

◆&nbsp;Expanding culture and tourism-linked content is crucial


