Chungcheongbuk-do&#39;s cultural project &quot;Dangsan Thinking Bunker&quot; concluded its 100-day schedule, which began in October last year and ended on the 31st of last month. The photo shows the interior view of the &quot;Lantern Room&quot; at the Dangsan Bunker. (Provided by Chungcheongbuk-do Provincial Government) ⓒCheonji Ilbo, May 12, 2025.



Projects such as Chungbuk&rsquo;s &ldquo;Lake Park Renaissance&rdquo; are increasing tourist inflows, boosting local consumption and employment. There are growing examples of reinterpreting traditional agriculture and local resources into cultural and tourism content, promoting sixth-industry innovation.

However, short average tourist stay, lack of integrated content, and low resident participation remain challenges. Professor Lee proposed expanding residential cultural programs, resident-led tourism models, and building an integrated tourism brand for Chungbuk as policy enhancements.

Kim Jae-jun (alias, 32, male, Cheongju), a video editor, said, &ldquo;Chungbuk is a proud place with semiconductor industries, but it lacks cultural infrastructure.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;The &lsquo;Thinking Bunker&rsquo; at Dangsan created through the &lsquo;Sea of Culture&rsquo; policy was a memorable and refreshing initiative,&rdquo; expressing hopes that cultural facilities and industries would continue to grow in the province.

◆ Cheongju Chamber of Commerce: &ldquo;Industrial diversification through private sector cooperation&rdquo;


<img alt="The photo shows Cheongju Mayor Lee Beom-seok (left) signing a memorandum of understanding (MOU) with Cha Tae-hwan, Chairman of the Cheongju Chamber of Commerce and Industry, on April 25, 2024. (Provided by Cheongju City Government)" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202505/3268145_3332029_424.jpg" />
The photo shows Cheongju Mayor Lee Beom-seok (left) signing a memorandum of understanding (MOU) with Cha Tae-hwan, Chairman of the Cheongju Chamber of Commerce and Industry, on April 25, 2024. (Provided by Cheongju City Government)



The Cheongju Chamber of Commerce and Industry (Cheongju CCI) emphasized the industrial significance of youth employment support and in-service welfare programs related to Chungbuk&rsquo;s industrial diversification.

Chairman Kim Tae-hwan explained, &ldquo;Cheongju CCI continues to supply field-ready talent to local industries through programs such as the Youth Challenge Support Project, Youth Work Experience Project, and Youth Future Plus Project.&rdquo;

He evaluated these as key policies that go beyond simple welfare measures and support Chungbuk&rsquo;s industrial restructuring. According to Cheongju CCI, the youth caf&eacute; has over 8,800 users, with about 500 employment beneficiaries. Onboarding training and youth job encouragement incentives have helped alleviate labor shortages by promoting long-term retention.

Kim emphasized, &ldquo;Industrial restructuring in Chungbuk requires both new industry promotion and talent development. Cheongju CCI will continue to support industry diversification in cooperation with local enterprises.&rdquo;

He also cited examples of local firms participating in AI, secondary batteries, and hydrogen industries, as well as private development projects linked to tourism and culture like the Danyang Station tourism facility, concluding that &ldquo;Chungbuk&rsquo;s industrial diversification must be completed through private sector cooperation and innovation.&rdquo;

◆ Chungbuk Province: &ldquo;We Will Establish a Step-by-Step Hydrogen Industry&rdquo;


<img alt="Chungcheongbuk-do Governor Kim Young-hwan delivers congratulatory remarks at the inauguration ceremony of the INNOBIZ Association Chungbuk Chapter held at the Enford Hotel in Cheongju on April 30. (Provided by Chungcheongbuk-do Provincial Government) ⓒCheonji Ilbo, May 12, 2025." src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202505/3268145_3332030_57.jpg" />
Chungcheongbuk-do Governor Kim Young-hwan delivers congratulatory remarks at the inauguration ceremony of the INNOBIZ Association Chungbuk Chapter held at the Enford Hotel in Cheongju on April 30. (Provided by Chungcheongbuk-do Provincial Government) ⓒCheonji Ilbo, May 12, 2025.



Starting this year, Chungbuk Province is launching its 2nd hydrogen industry development plan through 2030, aiming to build a clean hydrogen-based ecosystem. According to a response from the provincial government&rsquo;s spokesperson&rsquo;s office, &ldquo;The first phase focused on laying the industrial foundation with hydrogen vehicles, charging stations, and fuel cells.&rdquo;

They added, &ldquo;The second phase will aim to establish Chungbuk as a production and distribution hub for clean hydrogen.&rdquo; Plans include fostering the world&rsquo;s No.1 fuel cell production base for vehicles, building the largest hydrogen city in central Korea, and creating safety, standardization, education, and industrial hubs.

As Chungbuk seeks to overcome the economic crisis through industrial diversification this year, experts predict that real economic transformation will only be achieved when new industries, talent development, and private sector cooperation work in unison.
